00:10:55.055 --> 00:10:56.815
So back in 1989,

00:10:56.815 --> 00:10:58.175
Connecticut recognized

00:10:58.175 --> 00:11:01.695
that the state was having an affordable housing problem

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:05.510
and adopted Connecticut General Statute eight-thirty

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:06.150
gs,

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:09.190
which required each town to have 10%

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:11.030
of its housing

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.590
deemed affordable per the state definition.

00:11:16.005 --> 00:11:23.285
Andover is not the only town that struggled to meet that 10% wicket in Connecticut. And in fact, most

00:11:23.845 --> 00:11:29.710
of the smaller towns are in the same boat that Andover has been in with

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:31.870
much smaller number

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:34.430
of affordable homes than which are acquired.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:39.150
So Andover is subject to the provisions of eight thirty gs

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:44.670
because we're required to have the 10% of our housing units affordable and we're not.

00:11:46.725 --> 00:11:48.645
And what eight thirty gs

00:11:48.725 --> 00:11:51.045
specifies is that developers,

00:11:52.325 --> 00:11:58.245
when they come in and build the development, at least 30% of those units will be designated as affordable.

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:06.780
That is affordable to the people making less than 80% of the median income and only spending 30% of their

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.740
income on housing.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:14.540
And in fact, 15% of those that are designated as affordable

00:12:15.035 --> 00:12:19.195
will be affordable to families making 60% of the median income.

00:12:21.755 --> 00:12:23.755
In the significance of eight-thirty

00:12:23.755 --> 00:12:29.275
gs is it allows developers to disregard zoning rules while also shifting the burden of proof

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:34.590
to the town to justify a denial. So while our zoning regs require

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:40.030
about an acre and a half of land in 200 foot of frontage

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.030
and all these things to

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:42.990
make

00:12:43.845 --> 00:12:44.965
developments.

00:12:45.845 --> 00:12:47.845
Somebody coming in to develop

00:12:48.485 --> 00:12:50.405
under eight thirty gs can

00:12:50.645 --> 00:12:53.125
disregard all those requirements

00:12:53.525 --> 00:12:57.285
and build whatever kind of housing development they want,

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:01.030
as long as

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:03.750
it passes the public health code.

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:10.870
So it becomes very difficult for a town to deny an application which does not meet current zoning standards

00:13:11.035 --> 00:13:16.395
but does meet the minimum standards found in the act which says public health code has to buy into it.

00:13:17.595 --> 00:13:24.715
So essentially in order to deny an application the town is required to find documented and proven public health or safety concerns.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:33.350
The problem is that no matter how easy it is to gain approval for an affordable housing development,

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:37.990
the developer isn't going to develop the property unless he can make a profit in doing so.

00:13:46.155 --> 00:13:49.275
So little progress was made in getting the towns to

00:13:49.515 --> 00:13:50.715
create affordable housing.

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:53.510
And in 2017,

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:57.270
the Connecticut General Statute eight-30J

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:58.310
was adopted.

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:03.510
And this says that at least once every five years, each municipality

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:09.575
shall prepare or amend and adopt an affordable housing plan for the municipality.

00:14:10.535 --> 00:14:13.575
Such plan shall specify how the municipality

00:14:13.575 --> 00:14:17.735
intends to increase the number of affordable housing developments in the municipality.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:26.750
It doesn't say what needs to be in the plan, doesn't say what's required or who adopts it, and no public hearing is required.

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:35.185
So at this point, the Planning and Zoning Commission has drafted up this plan based on any feedback we get today.

00:14:35.425 --> 00:14:37.985
We might tweak on it a little bit but

00:14:38.145 --> 00:14:39.425
this is our plan.

00:14:41.585 --> 00:14:47.665
Last year Connecticut recognized it as an unfunded mandate to create the plan and use federal money

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:51.270
to create a grant program which he took

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.270
advantage of. We hired Bill Warner

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:58.310
and he created the plan with us. And these plans are due to the state

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:00.790
by the 06/01/2022,

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:03.030
which is coming up soon.

