00:19:00.745 --> 00:19:09.200
you know to have a deed restrict for ten years, we've got to provide incentive for the people that want to sign that. I don't know exactly how to do that but we'll figure it out.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:15.040
The other thing that was in the public act was that it allowed manufactured

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:16.320
mobile homes

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:19.600
that exceed 22 feet in their narrowest dimension

00:19:22.975 --> 00:19:24.015
in towns.

00:19:24.895 --> 00:19:27.855
Know, mobile homes have long been viewed as affordable

00:19:27.855 --> 00:19:28.975
housing distracting

00:19:28.975 --> 00:19:31.615
from the character that the towns are trying to maintain.

00:19:32.495 --> 00:19:33.615
And so Andover,

00:19:33.615 --> 00:19:34.415
like most

00:19:34.655 --> 00:19:40.790
small towns, currently prohibits mobile homes and mobile home parks, and we'll need to change that to be consistent

00:19:41.030 --> 00:19:42.870
with the Connecticut statute.

00:19:46.630 --> 00:19:48.310
Okay, so now we're going to look

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:49.590
at Andover

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:50.630
a little more

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:51.750
specifically.

00:19:53.185 --> 00:19:59.265
And this slide talks about the median household income by age of householder.

00:20:00.065 --> 00:20:05.905
And as we started down this path, I was concerned about the seniors. I know we have a lot of seniors in town

00:20:06.225 --> 00:20:07.505
living on a fixed income.

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:09.760
And as you can see,

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:12.320
for the

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:15.600
people 65 years and over,

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:20.640
the average income is down to around 73,000

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:21.440
per year,

00:20:22.195 --> 00:20:26.595
which is below the, you know, the 80% of the town median,

00:20:26.755 --> 00:20:28.355
which comes into about 81.

00:20:28.915 --> 00:20:32.115
The other thing that was interesting on this slide is

00:20:32.835 --> 00:20:33.955
we must not have

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:37.310
very many, if any households

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.870
with people 25

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:46.830
years of age in there because there's no data there at all. And you can see for overall Pollen County, know, the average,

00:20:47.505 --> 00:20:50.385
the median income there was like $26,000

00:20:50.385 --> 00:20:51.425
So that's

00:20:52.225 --> 00:20:58.065
pretty small, but certainly if we wanted to attract younger folks to start out in Andover,

00:20:58.705 --> 00:21:00.305
they're gonna need some help too.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:12.680
The state used median incomes in terms of family size.

00:21:13.255 --> 00:21:21.015
And I struggled to understand this at first because I was concerned about seniors living on fixed incomes and how did we take care of them

00:21:21.495 --> 00:21:26.215
and I think the lower senior incomes are reflected in the lower incomes for the smaller

00:21:26.215 --> 00:21:27.495
family sizes

00:21:27.495 --> 00:21:28.615
of one or two people.

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:35.200
Anyway, are the numbers that state uses in considering our eligibility

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:36.720
for affordable housing.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:42.800
Now let's look at Andover's

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:44.560
housing stock

00:21:45.255 --> 00:21:53.335
and I've got a bunch of statistics here and it probably gets blurred a little bit on what I'm trying to say but bear with me here.

00:21:53.655 --> 00:21:55.895
You know, we've got thirteen

00:21:56.135 --> 00:21:57.175
fifty seven

00:21:57.495 --> 00:21:59.495
housing units in Andover.

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:02.730
92%,

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:08.010
which is higher than the surrounding area's average, are single family homes.

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:11.290
And of those single family homes,

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:12.810
62%

00:22:13.235 --> 00:22:15.395
are three or more bedroom homes,

00:22:15.875 --> 00:22:19.475
which is our families are continuing to get smaller,

00:22:19.875 --> 00:22:20.355
maybe

00:22:21.315 --> 00:22:23.955
those aren't what people are looking for today.

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:27.200
82%

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:29.680
of our single family homes are owner occupied.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:31.920
And again, that's

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:34.640
a much higher number than the state average.

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:39.680
So we've only got 12% of those homes as rental units.

00:22:40.635 --> 00:22:45.755
And at the time the survey was taken, know, there were 7% or 87 units vacant.

00:22:47.835 --> 00:22:51.755
So anyhow, to have 10% of our housing affordable, we would require

00:22:52.075 --> 00:22:53.595
about 132

00:22:53.595 --> 00:22:55.710
housing units, which 10%

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:58.670
of that top number thirteen fifty seven.

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:05.150
We currently have 51 and this is a deficit of about 81 affordable homes.

00:23:09.665 --> 00:23:12.705
Now let's look at the rental market in Andover.

00:23:13.585 --> 00:23:19.025
For rental, the state programs use the fair market rent for the region is determined by HUD.

00:23:19.905 --> 00:23:26.040
And so in this little chart here in the center, can see the different fair market rents by unit bedroom

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:28.520
ranging from $670

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:31.560
a month for an efficiency up to about $1,500

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:33.320
a month for a four bedroom house.

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:39.480
Down below are the actual rents reported in Andover

00:23:40.385 --> 00:23:42.625
and you can see we've got 114

00:23:42.625 --> 00:23:43.425
units

00:23:43.825 --> 00:23:46.945
of $500 to $1,000 essentially,

00:23:47.265 --> 00:23:49.185
and that's the majority of our rental.

00:23:49.905 --> 00:23:53.830
Dollars 32 are in the $1,000 to $1,500

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:58.390
per month range and 10 are in excess of $1,500 a month.

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:03.430
So you look at the rent costs and you say gee whiz, Andover's

00:24:03.430 --> 00:24:04.550
a great place to live.

00:24:05.605 --> 00:24:14.165
As we saw from the last slide, you know, problem is there are a few rental options available to people in Andover and they're primarily older homes with more maintenance.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:19.990
Next, I wanted to look at the home ownership.

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:22.310
And this slide

00:24:23.030 --> 00:24:23.830
takes

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:30.390
two examples, a family of two and a family of four with the income that the state defined

00:24:30.745 --> 00:24:31.945
as being

00:24:33.625 --> 00:24:35.945
80% of the median for

00:24:36.185 --> 00:24:37.625
that family size.

00:24:39.145 --> 00:24:44.505
We're gonna take 30% of it to come up with an actual housing payment of

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:49.170
20,000 per year for a family of 2 or 24,000 for a family of four,

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:52.610
which comes down to a monthly payment of about $1,600

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:56.850
a month for a family of two and 2,000 for a family of four.

00:24:58.265 --> 00:25:01.625
Next we looked at what kind of house can you get for that kind of money

00:25:02.185 --> 00:25:04.825
and we looked at $250,000

00:25:04.825 --> 00:25:05.945
$300,000

00:25:05.945 --> 00:25:08.025
and $350,000

00:25:08.745 --> 00:25:13.680
And if you take the principal interest, what the taxes would be, add on the insurance,

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:16.320
that gives you a total payment for

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:18.400
a $250,000

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:20.800
house of about $15.80

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:21.600
dollars per month,

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:30.075
which we see that's what a family of two can afford. So that shows that a family of two can go in and get a $250,000

00:25:30.075 --> 00:25:30.715
house.

00:25:31.435 --> 00:25:35.355
That family of four can afford the $300,000

00:25:35.355 --> 00:25:35.995
house

00:25:36.715 --> 00:25:37.675
and

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:42.580
family of four didn't make it quite up to the $350,000

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:52.625
So this gets into what we call naturally

00:25:52.625 --> 00:25:54.705
occurring affordable units

00:25:54.865 --> 00:25:56.065
in Andover.

00:25:56.945 --> 00:26:02.625
As I mentioned before, to me, when I moved here, I thought Andover seemed to be a very affordable place to live.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:09.600
None of these homes have the forty year deed restrictions placed so they don't count towards affordable housing,

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:15.120
but you can see you know we've got the last four years worth of numbers here.

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:16.240
Total sales,

00:26:16.975 --> 00:26:21.695
how many of those sales were under $250,000

00:26:22.255 --> 00:26:24.335
How many were under $300,000

00:26:24.335 --> 00:26:27.935
and that includes those that were under $250,000

00:26:28.175 --> 00:26:30.415
And so it shows the percentage

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:33.200
of those that are under $300,000

00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:35.680
or affordable to a family of four

00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:37.760
ranging from 78%

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:38.880
in 2018

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:40.560
to 57%

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.000
in 2021.

00:26:44.805 --> 00:26:52.325
These homes are also older than average and while making them more affordable to buy, more costly to maintain, and less desirable than a newer home.

00:26:52.885 --> 00:26:58.325
You can also see the trend is toward fewer homes selling for the affordable prices

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:01.170
given the recent rapid increase in selling prices.

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:05.250
So that's where we are on our housing stock.

00:27:09.985 --> 00:27:15.985
This is a slide that really got my attention and should be to all of us living in Andover.

00:27:17.985 --> 00:27:20.145
It shows Andover's population

00:27:20.465 --> 00:27:21.265
going down.

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:33.740
And I thought this might be reflective of the statewide trend of population so I dug a little deeper into that and found that while the population in Connecticut increased slightly from 2019 to 2020

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:35.420
by about 1%,

00:27:36.215 --> 00:27:41.815
the population in Andover decreased by 87 people or 2.7%.

00:27:43.095 --> 00:27:46.695
I went back and looked at the last plan of conservation and development

00:27:46.775 --> 00:27:51.655
and that plan did not anticipate the decline in population. It showed it leveling off

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:54.310
but it did not recognize

00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:55.990
that it would decline.

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:57.270
That

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:01.030
document came out in 2015.

00:28:01.030 --> 00:28:02.710
You look at the numbers

00:28:02.870 --> 00:28:06.790
of the data that went into that, you know, from 2012 to 2014,

00:28:07.265 --> 00:28:11.585
it shows it kind of leveling off there and then we're starting to decline again.

00:28:13.585 --> 00:28:18.625
The Connecticut Data Center projects population to continue to decline further

00:28:18.865 --> 00:28:21.105
to only 2,864

00:28:21.105 --> 00:28:22.305
people in 2030

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:25.210
and 2,550

00:28:25.210 --> 00:28:26.490
in 2040.

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:33.530
Now we all think Andover is a great place to live, so why is this happening?

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:38.010
Should we be concerned and what can we do to change it if we think it's a problem?

00:28:39.805 --> 00:28:45.005
I go back to the recent long term plan that the town of Andover issued in 2019

00:28:45.245 --> 00:28:49.085
which recognized the need and importance for creating affordable housing.

00:28:49.965 --> 00:28:52.925
It said Andover will have to work hard to attract millennials,

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:54.380
especially homebuyers,

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:57.100
to overcome a bias toward more urban amenities.

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:01.660
It also went on to state that Andover has 93%

00:29:01.660 --> 00:29:03.100
single family housing.

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:06.300
This will need to change to attract younger demographic.

00:29:07.015 --> 00:29:11.335
This is coupled with the fact that Andover has very little affordable housing.

00:29:12.535 --> 00:29:13.495
So anyhow,

00:29:14.295 --> 00:29:19.830
we've recognized it as a problem like the state recognized it starting back in, you know,

00:29:20.470 --> 00:29:22.150
the late 1990s.

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:26.790
But I think it's something that should be a concern

00:29:26.870 --> 00:29:27.910
to all of us.

00:29:31.855 --> 00:29:33.135
Next slide

00:29:33.135 --> 00:29:39.615
shows the median age in Andover and it happens to be much older than the county and the state in general.

00:29:40.495 --> 00:29:42.175
This slide also got my attention.

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:47.660
We have recognized that Andover has an aging population

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:55.340
and again I attributed this to our country's population aging in general with the baby boomer population reaching our senior years.

00:29:57.045 --> 00:29:59.605
But Andover is aging faster than average.

00:30:00.405 --> 00:30:07.445
Do we have the housing to support them as they age and desire smaller houses on smaller lots that are easier to maintain?

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:12.960
Again from the long term plan the town issued in 2019,

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:15.200
Andover's

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:16.080
population

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:23.200
is aging and without a concerted effort to attract millennial homebuyers this trend will accelerate.

00:30:24.595 --> 00:30:27.475
And this is where my opinion on affordable housing

00:30:27.635 --> 00:30:30.435
shifted from something that was nice to do

00:30:30.755 --> 00:30:32.835
to something that we had to tackle

00:30:32.995 --> 00:30:35.155
for the future well-being of our town.

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:44.870
This slide shows the number of building permits issued per year

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:47.510
from about nineteen ninety

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:48.950
to twenty seventeen.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:52.870
We can see the building permits issued

00:30:53.465 --> 00:30:54.345
peaked

00:30:54.345 --> 00:30:56.105
in 1996

00:30:56.185 --> 00:30:59.305
at about 36 permits that year.

00:30:59.545 --> 00:31:00.025
Know,

00:31:00.425 --> 00:31:06.265
things were good, people were building lots of houses, people were buying, moving to Andover, and that sort of thing.

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:12.360
For the last ten to twelve years we have been seeing three to five building permits per year.

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:17.960
So the speculative market in housing

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:23.665
hasn't existed since the housing bubble burst back actually before 2010,

00:31:23.665 --> 00:31:26.305
probably 02/2008.

00:31:28.305 --> 00:31:36.060
With that, it's going to be difficult for Andover to reach the 10% goal. In the 90s, we were building 20 to 30 units of housing a year,

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:39.980
but from 2009 on, it's been less than 10 and

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:42.780
essentially three to five in the last several years.

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:50.385
We need 81 additional affordable units to meet our state mandate of 10% affordable.

00:31:51.025 --> 00:31:52.385
If we were to do this,

00:31:52.945 --> 00:31:55.585
and if a developer had to make 30%

00:31:56.305 --> 00:31:57.585
of a

00:31:57.665 --> 00:31:58.945
development affordable,

00:31:59.570 --> 00:32:07.890
that means we'd be building two seventy two new units in Andover, 191 at the market rate and 81 affordable.

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:16.045
And that's probably not going to happen anytime soon. So Andover will always be subject to that eight-thirty

00:32:16.045 --> 00:32:16.925
gs

00:32:17.485 --> 00:32:18.365
requirement.

00:32:21.485 --> 00:32:22.925
In a lot of towns,

00:32:23.485 --> 00:32:25.085
there's a very significant

00:32:25.670 --> 00:32:32.790
gap between the price that the affordable houses sell at and the price that are going at the market rate.

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:39.590
That allows those towns to make up for what they're losing on the affordable housing

00:32:39.275 --> 00:32:42.715
by the prices they're charging on the market rate units.

00:32:44.395 --> 00:32:46.955
That's not happening here in Andover.

00:32:47.035 --> 00:32:54.555
In a lot of the small towns, the gap is much narrower. We see if we want affordable housing to mix in well

00:32:55.100 --> 00:32:59.100
with the town, it's going to look very similar to the market

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:01.020
based

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:02.620
sales.

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:05.020
So this

00:33:05.900 --> 00:33:08.540
isn't going to solve our problem for us anytime soon.

00:33:09.615 --> 00:33:15.055
We've also got the lack of public sewer which makes density very challenging here in Andover.

00:33:16.175 --> 00:33:18.735
So while we're always going to be subject

00:33:19.215 --> 00:33:20.735
to the eight-thirty

00:33:20.735 --> 00:33:21.455
gs

00:33:21.615 --> 00:33:22.335
regulations,

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:27.120
the likelihood of a developer

00:33:28.400 --> 00:33:32.000
doing an eight thirty gs application in Andover is very small.

00:33:36.915 --> 00:33:38.755
Next, I wanted to look at who should

00:33:39.715 --> 00:33:41.235
the affordable housing

00:33:41.235 --> 00:33:42.435
plan target.

00:33:43.315 --> 00:33:47.715
And we've talked now about young folks wanting to keep them in town.

00:33:48.275 --> 00:33:57.200
And this graph of age distribution shows that we've got about four forty three people in the 10 19 year old age bracket.

00:33:57.440 --> 00:34:00.080
Now those folks aren't going go out and buy homes today,

00:34:00.640 --> 00:34:05.680
but by the time we get around to actually having some affordable housing, they may be in the marketplace

00:34:06.225 --> 00:34:10.385
and we'd be interested in keeping those people here in town.

00:34:12.305 --> 00:34:17.025
The other group that we talked about were the seniors and we see a big bracket

00:34:17.265 --> 00:34:18.225
from

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:22.680
50 to 69 years of age. We got two little spikes there

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:23.720
that

00:34:24.040 --> 00:34:27.160
show us we've got almost 1,200

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:29.080
empty nesters

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:32.040
potentially looking to downsize and stay in town.

00:34:33.075 --> 00:34:34.995
And if we don't have options

00:34:34.995 --> 00:34:39.235
for them to stay in town, that means that they're probably going to go someplace else.

00:34:42.835 --> 00:34:46.275
So with that, how many units are potentially

00:34:46.275 --> 00:34:46.675
needed?

00:34:48.990 --> 00:34:51.870
And somewhere off all the state data,

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:53.070
see that

00:34:53.470 --> 00:34:54.430
18%

00:34:54.430 --> 00:34:58.350
of the households in Andover spend between 3050%

00:34:58.350 --> 00:34:59.790
of their income on housing.

00:35:02.445 --> 00:35:07.725
On top of that, there's 4% of the households that spend more than half of their income on housing.

00:35:08.605 --> 00:35:11.645
So for this plan, you know, we took a look at

00:35:12.045 --> 00:35:16.125
the low end of what is needed, it would be the 4% that

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:17.690
spend more than half.

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:21.930
And if we went to the upper limit, it would be all

00:35:22.330 --> 00:35:25.930
the people that spend above 30% of their income on housing.

00:35:26.410 --> 00:35:28.490
And the same statistics said that

00:35:28.730 --> 00:35:31.885
the hourly wage needed to afford a two bedroom apartment

00:35:31.965 --> 00:35:34.525
is $23.65

00:35:35.965 --> 00:35:38.045
down below if we looked at the

00:35:38.365 --> 00:35:45.405
know the ten to nineteen year old age grouping there were four forty three residents. If we took care of the 4%

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:48.900
that are paying more than half that would be 18 residents.

00:35:49.060 --> 00:35:50.020
If we took

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:53.860
a look at the 18% that are between 30 50

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:55.380
that comes up to 80.

00:35:56.420 --> 00:36:02.020
Similarly with the fifty to sixty nine year olds, know we show that again 4%

00:36:02.315 --> 00:36:07.195
it comes out to about four fifty households when you take a look at there's about 2.6

00:36:07.195 --> 00:36:08.555
people per household.

00:36:09.835 --> 00:36:12.875
And again on the low end that would be about 18 households,

00:36:13.195 --> 00:36:22.160
the upper end 81 households. So that gives us a bracket of potential need anywhere from between thirty six and one hundred and sixty one units.

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:30.880
The need for affordable housing should not be viewed as something we need to do to meet the state requirement,

00:36:31.655 --> 00:36:36.375
but as we show here, something we want to tackle to get the young people to want to live here,

00:36:36.775 --> 00:36:39.895
raise a family here, retire here, and stay here.

00:36:40.695 --> 00:36:45.575
We really need to have more than our deficit of 81 units that's suggested.

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:52.820
So next we'll take a look at the affordable housing

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:55.140
units that currently exist.

00:36:57.220 --> 00:37:00.260
And again, we're supposed to have 10%,

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:02.420
which is 133

00:37:02.725 --> 00:37:04.085
units basically.

00:37:04.485 --> 00:37:05.845
In 2015,

00:37:06.805 --> 00:37:09.765
it shows that we had 24 government assisted

00:37:11.845 --> 00:37:13.925
units and 31

00:37:13.925 --> 00:37:17.525
units that were financed with the Connecticut Housing

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:21.360
Finance Authority or US Department of Agriculture

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:22.480
federally

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:23.360
subsidized

00:37:23.360 --> 00:37:24.240
loans

00:37:25.200 --> 00:37:30.960
for a total of 51 units. That comes out to an affordable rate of 4.18%

00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:32.880
as opposed to the 10% required.

00:37:34.995 --> 00:37:36.195
In 2020,

00:37:36.275 --> 00:37:39.875
we see that the numbers actually went down a little bit

00:37:40.835 --> 00:37:41.635
and

00:37:41.795 --> 00:37:50.390
I have not been able to get the light bulb on in my brain successfully on why the government assisted numbers went from 24 to 18.

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:55.110
I know there's still 24 units sitting over there on Riverside Drive.

00:37:55.750 --> 00:37:59.510
Talked to Eric, he tried to help explain that to me a little bit,

00:38:00.985 --> 00:38:07.545
but it just doesn't make sense that we should have lost credit for six of our affordable housing units there.

00:38:07.865 --> 00:38:08.745
But anyhow,

00:38:08.825 --> 00:38:09.625
you know,

00:38:10.985 --> 00:38:12.985
we did. I don't know if we can get it back,

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:17.900
but anyhow, that has resulted in our drop

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:23.580
in affordable housing numbers down to the total of 51 that we have today for 3.85%.

00:38:27.155 --> 00:38:28.995
So we need 81

00:38:28.995 --> 00:38:32.035
additional units to reach the 10%.

00:38:37.635 --> 00:38:39.955
That brings us to what should we have as a goal

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:42.920
in Andover for affordable housing?

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:45.240
And our plan came up with

00:38:45.720 --> 00:38:53.480
we should try to create five to 15 affordable housing units in the next ten years and it should be targeted to Andover seniors

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:55.240
interested in downsizing.

00:38:56.175 --> 00:39:03.535
In Andover's ten to nineteen year old age cohort, which I guess now is generation Z, the generation, the millennial generations,

00:39:04.015 --> 00:39:06.335
a little bit older than that now.

00:39:07.455 --> 00:39:10.575
And this sounds like a fairly modest goal in terms of what we need,

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:16.300
but could be a very challenging goal to meet in terms of what we have seen for building activity

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:17.820
over the past several years.

00:39:22.875 --> 00:39:29.355
This is where we get into talking about where is the property in Andover that we might be able to develop.

00:39:29.915 --> 00:39:34.635
And Andover put out an incentive housing zone study in 2017

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:39.290
and essentially came up with three possible sites that we could use.

00:39:40.730 --> 00:39:46.570
The first is the Phelps property over on Schottie Mill Road and it's about 9.4 acres that we could develop.

00:39:48.255 --> 00:39:53.855
The next property listed was Rosewood Acres on Lindholt's Corner which is about 16.4

00:39:53.855 --> 00:39:54.575
acres.

00:39:55.775 --> 00:40:00.255
And lastly is the town of Andover property behind the town hall

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:01.960
which is about 70 acres.

00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:04.920
And I wanted to take a little closer look

00:40:05.320 --> 00:40:09.960
at the town of Andover property as I consider this a very promising option

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:14.920
that because Andover already owns the property, we could make a deal with the developer

00:40:15.925 --> 00:40:19.365
to get the developer interested in building housing back there.

00:40:20.645 --> 00:40:25.525
The incentive housing zone created two development scenarios which are displayed in the following figures.

00:40:30.130 --> 00:40:31.250
And this shows

00:40:31.410 --> 00:40:35.250
you know several, this shows 10 of those multi unit

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:37.730
housing

00:40:38.850 --> 00:40:41.250
complexes that we saw in the picture right up front.

00:40:42.165 --> 00:40:43.685
42 units

00:40:43.925 --> 00:40:47.685
in 10 buildings with a mixture of one and two bedroom units

00:40:47.765 --> 00:40:50.805
and I thought that was a very attractive option back there.

00:40:53.925 --> 00:40:59.420
The other scenario that we looked through was your 24 single family lots of two bedroom homes.

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:07.820
And I think this would be a great move forward to the development of the town center concept that our plan of conservation development addresses,

00:41:08.140 --> 00:41:12.620
especially now that our new senior community center is approaching reality.

00:41:14.165 --> 00:41:18.805
This would be a great help to those in town who might benefit from increased affordable housing.

00:41:25.925 --> 00:41:27.285
So where do we go from here?

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:35.180
First, let's look at the organization awareness of the plan. Know, and the first goal

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:39.660
was to present the plan. I was hoping that we get a few more takers,

00:41:40.300 --> 00:41:42.940
but with the weather being so nice out there today, I know

00:41:43.885 --> 00:41:46.445
that's a tough sell to come listen to a presentation

00:41:47.085 --> 00:41:48.525
on affordable housing.

00:41:49.245 --> 00:41:50.125
But hopefully,

00:41:50.205 --> 00:41:50.685
you

00:41:51.245 --> 00:41:53.005
people that did attend can

00:41:53.165 --> 00:41:54.925
help the town out,

00:41:55.405 --> 00:41:59.090
spreading the word that, this is something we really need to do. And

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:00.370
I'm

00:42:00.370 --> 00:42:02.290
hoping that I get the opportunity

00:42:02.290 --> 00:42:04.210
to present the plan

00:42:04.370 --> 00:42:05.970
in additional venues

00:42:05.970 --> 00:42:07.330
going forward.

00:42:09.170 --> 00:42:12.770
It's important to recognize that the plan is not free,

00:42:13.275 --> 00:42:14.795
Doesn't cost very much,

00:42:15.515 --> 00:42:21.675
but as you can see from the third bullet down there, it's gonna talk about a permanent staff member

00:42:21.835 --> 00:42:23.435
being assigned to the committee.

00:42:23.675 --> 00:42:24.395
So

00:42:24.475 --> 00:42:28.395
we would have to pay for that. Any of the affordable housing that we developed

00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:30.360
in this plan

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:31.720
would add

00:42:32.680 --> 00:42:34.200
to the grant list,

00:42:34.840 --> 00:42:42.295
and so it would be a plus for the town budget that way, but it would certainly cost us a little bit to get started.

00:42:42.935 --> 00:42:46.855
Now, a couple of the things that we did not consider in our plan

00:42:47.175 --> 00:42:49.255
that other towns have considered,

00:42:50.055 --> 00:42:58.560
and they're actually putting in the budget the town purchasing property to make it available to developers so that they can go out and build affordable housing.

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:02.800
I didn't think that with as hard as we worked to get through

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:05.200
a budget to pass, that would be

00:43:05.600 --> 00:43:08.720
something that we wanted to address right now, so it's not in the plan.

00:43:09.635 --> 00:43:14.995
Other towns are also going out and buying housing that they are renting to people

00:43:15.395 --> 00:43:16.195
to

00:43:16.195 --> 00:43:19.075
create affordable housing there. With similar

00:43:19.075 --> 00:43:22.835
thing, I didn't think that was something that we would be looking at getting

00:43:24.070 --> 00:43:32.230
into our plan right now. However, if the town all of a sudden said, yep, those are great ideas, we should do them, be happy to add those into the plan.

00:43:34.390 --> 00:43:36.630
Next is create an affordable housing committee.

00:43:37.495 --> 00:43:44.295
Plans are often shelved because there's no champion. It is recommended that an affordable housing committee be established

00:43:44.455 --> 00:43:46.455
with the goal of keeping this plan

00:43:47.255 --> 00:43:48.135
in the forefront.

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:52.750
And we're hoping that the Board of Selectmen would be

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:54.750
willing to establish

00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:55.870
that committee.

00:43:57.790 --> 00:43:58.910
And ideally,

00:43:59.070 --> 00:43:59.710
a

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:08.845
permanent paid town staff member with true interest in this subject could be assigned to staff to the affordable housing committee to help them out.

00:44:13.165 --> 00:44:16.445
Next, look at what we would like the affordable housing committee to do.

00:44:18.270 --> 00:44:28.670
And the committee and the staff could use social media, print media, and presentations to highlight the need and identify and work with residents in need of affordable housing or interested

00:44:28.670 --> 00:44:32.590
in renovations to agent plays to create affordable accessory

00:44:32.590 --> 00:44:33.070
units.

00:44:35.565 --> 00:44:40.205
Perhaps one of the biggest issues to buying a house is lack of knowledge of existing programs.

00:44:40.685 --> 00:44:45.245
The committee and staff would work with REALTORS to promote the Connecticut Housing Finance Authority

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:50.080
and U. S. Department Of Agriculture mortgage and down payment assistance programs.

00:44:50.720 --> 00:44:52.720
Homes bought with these programs

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:55.200
count as state defined affordable housing.

00:45:00.235 --> 00:45:08.875
The general statutes municipal powers allow the creation of a housing trust fund. All towns in Connecticut are in a unique position they have found money

00:45:09.115 --> 00:45:10.955
in the form of COVID relief funds.

00:45:11.640 --> 00:45:15.000
And I recognize that most of the money is going towards building

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:19.000
the new senior community center and I completely agree with this.

00:45:19.400 --> 00:45:34.245
It's recommended that a housing trust fund be established with an initial deposit from the COVID relief funds if there's any remaining and subsequent deposits could come from the annual budget if we chose to fund that in there or end of the year transfers of surplus funds.

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:41.210
We should be applying for small cities community

00:45:41.210 --> 00:45:42.890
development block grants,

00:45:43.610 --> 00:45:47.450
funding for money to fund the housing trust fund for housing rehabilitation

00:45:47.450 --> 00:45:48.650
and modification

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:51.210
for low and moderate income owners and tenants.

00:45:53.155 --> 00:45:55.715
In considering Andover's age demographics,

00:45:55.715 --> 00:46:01.075
helping seniors comfortably age in place will be a huge issue to avoid social isolation.

00:46:01.475 --> 00:46:10.820
A safe and affordable home is critical, but also to assist seniors with aging in place it's critical that the town continues to provide support programs to maintain

00:46:10.980 --> 00:46:12.580
a good quality of life.

00:46:12.980 --> 00:46:14.980
These include the senior centers

00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:17.940
programming for all types of seniors,

00:46:17.940 --> 00:46:20.980
meals on wheels, dial a ride, visiting nurse program,

00:46:21.455 --> 00:46:24.895
and handyman services for small jobs within their homes.

00:46:26.735 --> 00:46:27.295
To

00:46:27.695 --> 00:46:28.655
help further

00:46:28.895 --> 00:46:33.855
affordable housing, we should consider tax abatement programs as allowed under the general

00:46:34.175 --> 00:46:35.375
Connecticut statutes.

00:46:40.910 --> 00:46:44.430
Next, we should be looking at modifying the existing housing stock.

00:46:45.950 --> 00:46:47.630
A recent AARP

00:46:47.950 --> 00:46:50.190
survey found eighty percent of seniors

00:46:50.565 --> 00:46:52.405
would like to remain in their homes

00:46:52.645 --> 00:46:54.005
and age in place.

00:46:54.405 --> 00:46:57.445
Consider direct grants from the housing trust fund

00:46:57.765 --> 00:47:01.205
and the small cities eligible grants to encourage

00:47:01.205 --> 00:47:04.565
low to moderate income qualified seniors to modify their homes

00:47:05.160 --> 00:47:07.080
to allow aging in place.

00:47:08.440 --> 00:47:09.000
This

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:12.920
might be single floor living, bathroom modifications,

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:13.560
ramps,

00:47:14.120 --> 00:47:17.560
and or to create affordable accessory units for extra income

00:47:18.200 --> 00:47:20.120
from a relative or a caregiver.

00:47:23.055 --> 00:47:24.895
And the housing

00:47:26.015 --> 00:47:27.135
trust fund

00:47:27.295 --> 00:47:33.535
that could be paid back for many loans that we give out when the house is eventually sold by

00:47:33.935 --> 00:47:35.695
the people getting the loans.

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:45.270
We could create a program which creates affordable accessory dwelling units by creating a direct grant housing trust fund in small cities,

00:47:45.590 --> 00:47:47.110
community development grant,

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:50.150
block grant eligible or tax abatements.

00:47:51.175 --> 00:48:01.895
To ease the process, we could have available maximum rental rates and model ten year deed restriction acceptable to the State Department of Housing to qualify as affordable units

00:48:02.215 --> 00:48:04.615
and help with our annual reporting process.

00:48:07.080 --> 00:48:10.920
We should look at expanding the senior housing over on Riverside Drive.

00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:17.640
There are currently 24 units at Riverside Drive Senior Housing. There is currently a waiting list

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:23.445
and like other senior housing, it may be a couple years before a unit will become available.

00:48:23.925 --> 00:48:29.605
It is anticipated that the lack of senior housing units will only get worse over time.

00:48:30.805 --> 00:48:36.245
And this is exactly at the time when more and more seniors are realizing they need more affordable housing

00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:38.700
and do not want to leave their hometowns.

00:48:39.500 --> 00:48:45.420
The town should work with management at Riverside Drive Senior Housing to evaluate the feasibility of expansion.

00:48:45.820 --> 00:48:49.100
There are state and federal funds to assist with such expansions.

00:48:51.365 --> 00:48:57.525
And lastly, should make sure that seniors in need are aware of the statute

00:48:57.525 --> 00:48:59.765
on temporary health care structures.

00:49:00.005 --> 00:49:01.925
And what this allows is,

00:49:02.085 --> 00:49:03.365
you know, essentially

00:49:03.460 --> 00:49:11.060
a small mobile home to be brought on the property for the duration of the need that a caregiver might live in if

00:49:11.140 --> 00:49:12.980
the property owner chose to go that way.

00:49:17.795 --> 00:49:21.235
And there are a couple of things for the Planning and Zoning Commission to do.

00:49:21.875 --> 00:49:28.035
First, we should adopt the affordable housing plan as an addendum to our current plan of conservation and development,

00:49:28.675 --> 00:49:33.075
and we'll be doing that soon because we owe this plan to the state by

00:49:32.730 --> 00:49:33.850
June 1.

00:49:34.730 --> 00:49:36.810
We'll recommend to the Board of Selectmen

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:40.810
that we opt out of the requirements of Public Act 2,129

00:49:40.810 --> 00:49:42.650
for accessory dwelling units.

00:49:43.530 --> 00:49:47.290
This is recommended because complying could be detrimental to Andover Lake.

00:49:49.315 --> 00:49:54.755
After opting out, we can amend the regulations to comply with those things we like in the act

00:49:54.915 --> 00:49:57.395
in the Andover Rural Design Zone.

00:49:57.795 --> 00:49:59.075
Most significantly,

00:49:59.475 --> 00:50:06.440
as of rate units up to a thousand square feet and remove the special permit requirements for detached units.

00:50:06.920 --> 00:50:12.040
Right now somebody has to come in and get a special permit with a public hearing to put up

00:50:13.105 --> 00:50:28.145
unit on their house, which most people would put in the upstairs of a barn or a garage or something like that. We think that might be one of the things we can do that would entice people to sign up for that ten year deed restriction to make that count as affordable housing.

00:50:30.520 --> 00:50:32.600
We can amend the Andover Rural

00:50:33.320 --> 00:50:34.600
Design District

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:38.040
to allow as of right two to four unit structures

00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:39.560
on larger lots.

00:50:40.200 --> 00:50:46.495
And in this we would include design guidelines to protect the rural character of our town and the single residential

00:50:46.655 --> 00:50:47.775
family character.

00:50:48.495 --> 00:50:52.975
This will increase the supply and diversity of housing stock with smaller, more affordable

00:50:53.295 --> 00:50:54.495
housing units

00:50:54.495 --> 00:50:56.255
and they all add to our grant list.

00:50:57.550 --> 00:51:05.230
And we'll need to eliminate the mobile homes and mobile home parks meeting the definition of the state statute from our prohibited use section.

00:51:07.550 --> 00:51:10.510
And lastly, we should amend section 4.14,

00:51:11.055 --> 00:51:19.695
planned residential development for older persons to eliminate the need for special permits and add a density bonus in exchange for affordable units.

00:51:20.175 --> 00:51:22.015
We need to do everything we can

00:51:22.895 --> 00:51:23.935
to help

00:51:24.590 --> 00:51:28.190
make developers want to come in and develop the town here.

00:51:29.390 --> 00:51:36.510
We can amend the Andover mixed use floating zone to incorporate an optional affordable component in exchange for higher density

00:51:36.510 --> 00:51:38.270
in a more straightforward,

00:51:38.270 --> 00:51:39.870
less risky approval process.

00:51:43.305 --> 00:51:51.945
We should adopt the incentive housing zone, perhaps increasing the density in recognition that the State Department of Health septic capacity has recently increased

00:51:52.520 --> 00:51:55.480
from 5,000 gallons per day to 7,500

00:51:55.480 --> 00:52:01.480
gallons per day for the septic system, making it easier to put more dense housing in.

00:52:02.840 --> 00:52:11.095
And we should proactively rezone the three incentive housing zones discussed in the affordable housing plan using an overlay zone technique

00:52:11.175 --> 00:52:13.575
which preserves the existing zoning

00:52:13.735 --> 00:52:15.895
but would allow the incentive housing

00:52:16.215 --> 00:52:21.095
zone development as of right if a developer were to come in and try to do that.

00:52:23.230 --> 00:52:27.790
After rezoning the property, the Planning and Zoning Commission with the affordable housing committee

00:52:27.950 --> 00:52:32.990
can advocate that the town seriously consider opening discussions with for profit

00:52:33.070 --> 00:52:40.685
and not for profit developers who might be interested in development of a neighborhood on the town property adjacent to the Town Hall Fire Station,

00:52:41.005 --> 00:52:43.485
including senior and affordable units.

00:52:47.645 --> 00:52:48.925
So in conclusion,

00:52:49.725 --> 00:52:52.765
affordable housing is a good thing

00:52:52.530 --> 00:52:55.090
and a necessary thing for our down future.

00:52:56.050 --> 00:52:59.250
There is a growing need for affordable housing in the Andover.

00:52:59.890 --> 00:53:04.850
There's a lot of grant money available right now to support affordable housing efforts.

00:53:05.885 --> 00:53:09.725
There are steps Andover can take to encourage affordable housing development.

00:53:10.765 --> 00:53:15.885
And affordable housing development is already well underway in many other towns.

00:53:16.045 --> 00:53:17.405
You know, was a

00:53:18.590 --> 00:53:22.510
article in the paper I think a week ago talked about Glastonbury

00:53:22.510 --> 00:53:23.390
building

00:53:23.550 --> 00:53:24.590
1,000

00:53:24.670 --> 00:53:25.710
apartments

00:53:25.710 --> 00:53:28.190
on the corner of Route 83 And 94 there.

00:53:30.405 --> 00:53:34.485
So I know we need some champions to lead the effort. I know you're out there.

00:53:35.685 --> 00:53:36.805
And at this point,

00:53:37.205 --> 00:53:41.045
all those champions may not hear today, but I'm interested in hearing what you think.

00:53:42.900 --> 00:53:44.740
So Diane or Joanne,

00:53:45.300 --> 00:53:46.420
what do you think?

00:53:50.820 --> 00:53:52.260
>> I'll go first.

00:53:52.820 --> 00:53:59.235
I'm very impressed Jed, can you guys hear me okay? This computer sometimes has issues. Nope, hear you fine.

00:53:59.475 --> 00:54:00.195
Okay,

00:54:00.435 --> 00:54:01.955
yeah, I'm very impressed.

00:54:02.115 --> 00:54:06.035
As someone that has a hobby of real estate and

00:54:06.275 --> 00:54:07.715
things of this nature,

00:54:08.195 --> 00:54:09.635
I was very, you know,

00:54:11.140 --> 00:54:16.980
Engrossed in learning, you know, expanding my knowledge base about a lot of issues that you brought up.

00:54:17.380 --> 00:54:21.860
You know, maybe when it first started coming up in the board of selectman meetings,

00:54:22.340 --> 00:54:22.820
I was like,

00:54:23.415 --> 00:54:26.375
you know, I don't know if that's a topic we need to really,

00:54:26.775 --> 00:54:28.295
you know, dive into.

00:54:28.855 --> 00:54:31.975
Although I knew, you know, you and Eric

00:54:32.135 --> 00:54:37.990
were supporting it and bringing it forward month after month. But with recent events with inflation

00:54:38.150 --> 00:54:44.070
and all the documentation you gave us here today with the trends with

00:54:44.390 --> 00:54:46.390
just the housing prices

00:54:46.870 --> 00:54:49.110
and also with the

00:54:49.645 --> 00:54:51.405
age and the population,

00:54:52.285 --> 00:54:53.565
you know, going down.

00:54:54.285 --> 00:55:01.165
Very scary with everything the town is trying to do right now and then seeing those trends. So that really opened my eyes.

00:55:01.970 --> 00:55:02.690
So

00:55:03.170 --> 00:55:05.730
I say why not Andover

00:55:05.890 --> 00:55:07.970
for those identifiable

00:55:07.970 --> 00:55:09.490
pieces of property

00:55:09.890 --> 00:55:12.530
that you see having those nice units

00:55:12.690 --> 00:55:13.890
that other towns have,

00:55:14.605 --> 00:55:15.325
you know,

00:55:15.645 --> 00:55:18.525
I more so go for those standalone,

00:55:18.525 --> 00:55:19.965
maybe two bedroom

00:55:20.445 --> 00:55:21.165
places,

00:55:21.725 --> 00:55:28.845
you know, I really do worry about our seniors and everything that they need. They kind of are the backbone of the community

00:55:29.180 --> 00:55:31.980
and they support everything else in town.

00:55:32.540 --> 00:55:35.020
So why not, you know, with the new

00:55:35.340 --> 00:55:38.300
senior slash community center being built,

00:55:38.620 --> 00:55:41.740
why not look at that property back there to have some affordable,

